            %h3#initial_purpose_of_page Initial Purpose of Page
            %p
              The ideas on this page are things that I,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Stacey"} Andrew Stacey
          .query
            %p
              So should we merge this with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PRO"} PRO
              now? —Toby
            %p Yes. However, not knowing what is standard and what isn’t, I’m not sure I could do that merge. The stuff on this page is what I want to know about so I don’t want to lose any of it in the merge. —Andrew
            %p Understood. I'm not sure how to do it either, but I'll think about it. —Toby
            %p
              I should say that if any of this
              %em doesn’t
              fit in the merge then I’m happy for it to find a home elsewhere. My main purpose in scribbling this lot is to find out what is and isn’t known so I don’t have to reprove the known stuff just to make use of it. —Andrew
            %p Now I want to wait until Todd figures out what you really want to do here. (^_^) —Toby
          %h3#the_basic_idea The Basic Idea
          %p
            The idea is that one tries to “do”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+algebra"} universal algebra
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            by using the monoidal products in place of the standard cartesian ones. The extent to which this is possible is closely related to the extent to which the monoidal product resembles the cartesian product of sets.
          %h3#in_more_detail In More Detail
          %p
            Let us consider only finitary theories; that is, with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theories"} Lawvere theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            with finite cartesian products in which every object is isomorphic to a finite cartesian power of a distinguished object. A model for that theory in some category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is then a product-preserving functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \to C
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            %em monoidal
            functor.
          %p
            The problem with this naive definition is that as the product in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %strong is
            the cartesian product, it comes along with a whole heap of extra structure that may not be strictly needed. We therefore need to weaken the definition of a Lawvere theory.
          #monlawth.num_defn{:style => "border:solid #cccccc;border-width:2px 1px;padding:0 1em;margin:0 1em;"}
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong monoidal Lawvere theory
              is a monoidal category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (T, \otimes, I)
              in which every object is isomorphic to a finite monoidal product of some distinguished object.
          %p
            It will be important for later to note what is missing between a
            %strong monoidal
            Lawvere theory and an ordinary one. The missing components are:
          %ol
            %li
              No projection morphisms; e.g.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^n \to x
            %li
              No diagonal morphisms; e.g.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \to x^n
            %li
              No symmetry morphisms; e.g.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^2 \to x^2
          %p With this definition, we can define a model of such a theory in a monoidal category.
          #model.num_defn{:style => "border:solid #cccccc;border-width:2px 1px;padding:0 1em;margin:0 1em;"}
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C, \otimes, I)
              be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (T, \otimes, I)
              be a monoidal Lawvere theory. A
              %strong
                monoidal
                = succeed "-object" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C,\otimes, I)
              is a monoidal functor
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \to C
          %h3#the_trade The Trade
          %p Any Lawvere theory is automatically a monoidal Lawvere theory and so one can consider monoidal models of an ordinary Lawvere theory in a monoidal category. However, as ordinary Lawvere theories have all the extra structure of projections, diagonals, and symmetry, the underlying object (i.e. the image of the distinguished object in the Lawvere theory) needs to have them as well. Indeed, if one were looking for candidates for such models one would be well-advised to look in the subcategories of objects which have such structure. Let us therefore give these names.
          #cartcats.num_defn{:style => "border:solid #cccccc;border-width:2px 1px;padding:0 1em;margin:0 1em;"}
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C, \otimes, I)
              be a monoidal category. We define the following full subcategories of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %ol
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} sym
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\operatorname{sym}}
                is the largest
                %strong symmetric
                monoidal subcategory. That is, it is the full subcategory where the switcheroo functor is naturally isomorphic to the identity.
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} co
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\operatorname{co}}
                is the subcategory of commutative, associative, unital
                = succeed "." do
                  %strong comonoids
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .query
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+Trimble"}> Todd
              \: Sorry, I don’t know what you mean by the switcheroo functor for a monoidal category.
            %p
              There is what is called the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/center"} center
              of a monoidal category, which is a braided monoidal category. The center of the center should be symmetric monoidal (although I’d have to think this through again carefully). Comonoids in the center or center of center form a monoidal category, and I’m thinking something like this would be a natural habitat to consider.
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Stacey"}> Andrew
              \: Knowing that none of my terminology would be right, I let myself get carried away there. Apologies. I mean the functor that swaps the order of the product, namely that takes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \otimes B
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \otimes A
            %p Or maybe I can’t. Now I write it, it seems a lot more tenuous than when I thought it. It’d be useful to get that clear.
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+Trimble"}> Todd
              \: Not sure I can add anything else useful right now. I don’t think there’s a general switcheroo functor. In some sense a monoidal functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Fin
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fin^{op} \to M
              to a monoidal category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              ought to simulate a cocommutative comonoid in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %p Is there a specific setting you have in mind where what you want to do is unproblematic?
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Stacey"}> Andrew
              \: Yes. I want to do it in the category of models in set of a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebraic+theory"} commutative algebraic theory
            %p (As a little background: I’m returning to some stuff that I thought about a while ago and am trying to finish off, but I don’t remember all of what I thought when I originally thought about it which is why I’m not as clear as I would like to be)
            %p
              %em> Toby
              \: Even in a symmetric monoidal category, I don't know how to define a functor that takes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \otimes B
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \otimes A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ×
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \times C \to C
              that takes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,B)
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \otimes A
            %p
              There's an inherent problem with considering the largest (or any) symmetric subcategory of a given monoidal category, which is that being symmetric is not a property but a structure on a monoidal category. So it's more like looking for the largest commutative submonoid of a given
              %em set
              than looking for the largest commutative submonoid of a given monoid.
            %p
              Even if that worked, I wouldn't understand the full subcategory of CAU comonoids, since again being a CAU comonoid is not a property but a structure on an object in a given symmetric monoidal category. This is like asking for the full subcategory of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              consisting of groups; it doesn't parse. At best, I would understand this as the full subcategory of sets that have at least one group structure, but you're no longer keeping track of that structure (and in fact, at least assuming the axiom of choice, this is just
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                      %mo ∖
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi ∅
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set \setminus \{\empty\}
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Stacey"}> Andrew
              \: Okay, let me be absolutely specific here. I was trying to get at the general story first, but that’s the wrong way round for
              %em here
              (it’s the right way round for the paper, but that’s once I’ve understood what the general story is!).
            %p
              This is tied up with
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tall-Wraith+monoids"} Tall-Wraith monoids
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Ab
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Ab, \otimes, \mathbb{Z})
            %ul
              %li
                %em> Toby
                \: OK, this makes sense. In general, you start with a comonoid in your monoidal category; a comonoid in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Ab
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Ab,\otimes)
                is a coalgebra, while a comonoid in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Set
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ×
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Set,\times)
                is simply a set (by an argument that applies whenever the monoidal category is cartesian).
            %p Furthermore, if I wish to interpret the resulting biring as representing a functor from abelian groups to rings, then I can only do so on the subcategory of coalgebras - but I need to take abelian group homs not coalgebra homs.
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %em> Toby
                  \: H'm, this is odd. I still don't know exactly what the ‘subcategory’ of coalgebras is, since being a coalgebra is not a property of an abelian group. Since the forgetful functor from coalgebras to abelian groups is faithful, you can make it a literal
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategory"} subcategory
                  up to equivalence, then consider the corresponding full subcategory; or (equivalently) just take the category whose objects are coalgebras and whose morphisms are homomorphisms of the underlying abelian groups. (In general, this concept is the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+image"} full image
                  of a functor; here we are taking the full image of the forgetful functor from coalgebras to abelian groups.)
                %p But I don't understand why you need to use this instead of simply the category of coalgebras. After all, if you have a functor from the full image, then you certainly have a functor from the category of coalgebras by composing with the inclusion functor of coalgebras into the full image. On the other hand, it seems very strange that you can define this functor on arbitrary abelian group homomorphisms. Applying this to abelian group isomorphisms, you are basically saying that, if you start with two different coalgebra structures on a given abelian group, the resulting birings will be isomorphic. Is that true?
                %p
                  Andrew — Okay, I still wasn’t being completely clear. I
                  %em do
                  mean the category of coalgebras. But the
                  %em functor
                  is constructed by taking the composition of the functor from coalgebras to abelian groups and
                  %em then
                  taking the hom-functor
                  %em in abelian groups
                  with a biring. So if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                  is a coalgebra and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                  is a biring then I want to consider
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi Hom
                            %mi Ab
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi C
                          %mo ,
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom_{Ab}(C,B)
                  %em coalgebra
                  morphisms.
                %p Phew! “Oh what a tangeled web we weave, when first we practise to understand category theory.”
                %p
                  %em> Toby
                  \: OK, so when you wrote ‘I need to take abelian group homs not coalgebra homs’, you weren't still specifying the source of the functor but were instead specifying how that biring
                  %em represents
                  the functor. Now it makes sense!
            %p
              This all generalises quite nicely: replace abelian groups by a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebraic+theory"} commutative algebraic theory
              and replace rings (rather, replace monoids) by a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PRO"} PRO
            %p
              But this
              %em felt
              like it was a specialisation of an even more general story. My mistake was to try to guess the general story first rather than tell you the special case.
            %p
              (Not that I regard this particularly as a
              = succeed "!" do
                %em mistake
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PRO"} PROs
              and it’s all part of learning how to ask good questions.)
            %p
              %em> Todd
              \: Okay, so to pick up the thread again, here is a preliminary observation. Suppose we have an operad
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              (giving rise to a prop
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Pr
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pr(P)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              (such as the category of abelian groups, or the category of algebras of a commutative monad on
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi Pr
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A: Pr(P) \to K
              = succeed "-coalgebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo :
                      %mi Pr
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi P
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi op
                      %mo →
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C: Pr(P)^{op} \to K
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_1
              denote the underlying
              = succeed "-objects" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              at tensor-generating object 1 of
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Pr
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pr(P)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(-, -)
              denote internal hom in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(C_1, A_1)
              whose operations
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi θ
                      %mo ^
                    %mo :
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{\theta}: K(C_1, A_1)^{\otimes n} \to K(C_1, A_1)
            %p are obtained as composites
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi C
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %msubsup
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi θ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi θ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(C_1, A_1)^{\otimes n} \to K(C_{1}^{\otimes n}, A_{1}^{\otimes n}) \overset{K(C(\theta), A(\theta))}{\to} K(C_1, A_1)
            %p
              where the first map is a canonical map available in any symmetric monoidal closed category. If we denote this
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(C, A)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actegory"} actegory
              action
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Coalg
                      %mi P
                      %mi op
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi Alg
                      %mi P
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Alg
                      %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(-, -): Coalg_P^{op} \times Alg_P \to Alg_P
            %p
              and in this framework one could go on to consider
              = succeed "-bialgebras," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CommCoalg"} CommCoalg
              or whatever we’re calling it now. I don’t know how well it generalizes to general props or pros.
          %p
            Thus to be able to consider
            %em arbitrary
            Lawvere theories we need to be in the intersection of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} sym
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\operatorname{sym}}
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} co
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\operatorname{co}}
          %p
            If one is given a presentation of a Lawvere theory in terms of operations and identities, it is quite simple to identify what level of structure it requires. The key is to look at the identities and express them in terms of elements (this, of course, violates the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            %em distinct
            elements.
          %ol
            %li If an identity involves changing the order of the elements, it needs the symmetric structure.
            %li If an identity involves duplicating an element, it needs the diagonal structure.
            %li If an identity involves ignoring an element, it needs the projection structure.
          %h3#a_worked_example A Worked Example
          %p
            The passage from the theory of unital, associative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
            to that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            is a good illustration of all of this. The Lawvere theory of unital, associative monoids has a presentation with 2 operations: one zeroary,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
          %ol
            %li
              Associativity
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\mu(a,\mu(b,c)) = \mu(\mu(a,b),c)
            %li
              Unit
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\mu(a,1) = a, \qquad \mu(1,a) = a
          %p
            None of these identities involve swaps, repetitions, or omissions and so this presentation is also of a monoidal Lawvere theory (note that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is an operation not an element).
          %p
            Now let us add in an inverse. That is, a unary operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ι
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota
            with the identities:
          %ol
            %li
              Inverses
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ι
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mo =
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ι
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\mu(\iota(a),a) = 1, \qquad = \mu(a, \iota(a)) = 1
          %p
            These involve both a duplication and an omission. The duplication is on the left-hand side in each case, the omission on the right. Note that the starting point for each is the ordered list
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a)
            %strong group
            object in a monoidal category must lie in the subcategory of comonoids.
          %p Let us forget the inverse for a moment and add in the commutativity identity instead. This is the identity:
          %ol
            %li
              Commutativity
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\mu(a,b) = \mu(b,a)
          %p
            This obviously involves a swap, and thus requires a
            %em symmetric
            object as its model.
          %p Finally, for an abelian group object we need both: symmetry and comonoid.
          %h3#representing_functors Representing Functors
          %p
            One of the applications of Lawvere theories is to the theory of representing objects. Under mild conditions, a model of a Lawvere theory is equivalent to a lift of a representable contravariant functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to \operatorname{Set}
            to a functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to \operatorname{Set}T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \operatorname{Set}T
            is the category of models of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \operatorname{Set}
          %p
            However, where it does work is when one restricts to the subcategory of comonoid objects
            = succeed "." do
              %em with comonoid-structure preserving morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} co
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\operatorname{co}}_{c}
            denotes the subcategory of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            of comonoids with such morphisms then a model
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo ,
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C,\otimes,I)
            represents a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} co
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\operatorname{co}}_{c} \to \operatorname{Set}T
            via
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} co
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi Hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C^{\operatorname{co}}_c \to C \overset{Hom(-,G)}{\to} \operatorname{Set}T
          %h3#questions Questions
          %p Apart from the obvious question of finding the literature behind all of this (and thus getting the terminology correct), certain other questions spring to mind. Well, okay, one question springs to mind.
          %ol
            %li
              Suppose that I have two monoidal Lawvere theories,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_2
              = succeed ")," do
                %sup#fnref:1
                  %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
              %strong monoidal
              Lawvere theories equivalent?
          %p
            %em> Todd
            \: My previous answer (which has been deleted) was based on a too-hasty reading of the question. I’ll try to set aside some time to find a better answer.
          %p
            %em> Todd
            \: The time set aside turned out to be time last night when I had trouble falling asleep. I believe the answer is no. If for example
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1
            is the pro for comonoids and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_2
            is the initial pro (so equivalent to the natural numbers as discrete monoidal category), then the “Lawverifications” of those pros ought to be equivalent, both being equivalent to the initial Lawvere theory
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi FinSet
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinSet^{op}
          %h3#references References
          %p
            S. Lack, Composing PROPS,
            %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/13/9/13-09abs.html"} Theory Appl. Categ.
            13 (2004), No. 9, 147–163.
          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  I keep wanting to write “repetition, hesitation, or deviation”.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:1", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
